  {
    id:'opqb-t23-30', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-glauc',
    stem:'The main action of intraocular pressure (IOP) is to:',
    options:['Maintain globe shape','Control aqueous production','Regulate corneal thickness','Protect retina from ischemia'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:'**The eye is a fluid-filled sphere, and only pressure keeps a fluid-filled sphere in shape.**\n\nThe globe’s optical performance depends on its **geometry** being constant: the cornea at a fixed curvature, the lens at a fixed distance behind it, and the retina at a fixed distance behind that. The sclera is tough but not rigid, so what actually holds those distances is the **outward push of the aqueous and vitreous against the coats** — the intraocular pressure. Drop the pressure far enough (**hypotony**, after a penetrating injury, over-filtering surgery or ciliary shutdown) and the consequences are all geometric: **corneal folds, a shallow anterior chamber, choroidal detachment, hypotony maculopathy with chorioretinal folds**, and a measurable **refractive shift** — the eye literally changes shape and the image goes out of focus. ⚠️ **This reasoning is __not taken from the course material__:** grepping the 29 cached decks and the book caches for a statement of the *function* of IOP returned nothing; the nearest printed statement is in `ophthalmology.pdf`’s anatomy chapter, where the **vitreous** is said to **"help preserve the shape of the globe"** — the same idea attributed to the contents rather than to their pressure.\n\n**What the module does print about IOP.** `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` gives the **"normal range 10–21 mmHg (mean = 16 mmHg)"** and the methods of measuring it; the glaucoma decks treat it purely as the modifiable risk factor for optic neuropathy. The bank is asking the physiological question underneath all of that.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Control aqueous production** | ⚠️ Reverses cause and effect. **Aqueous production and outflow generate the pressure**, not the other way round: the ciliary epithelium secretes at a roughly fixed rate and the trabecular meshwork resists outflow, and IOP is the result of that balance. There is only a weak pressure-dependent brake on secretion; it is not a "main action". |\n| **Regulate corneal thickness** | The opposite of the true relationship in two ways. Corneal thickness is maintained by the **endothelial pump**, and central corneal thickness **distorts the measurement of IOP** rather than being controlled by it — a thick cornea reads falsely high, a thin one falsely low. Very high pressure overwhelms the pump and causes epithelial **oedema** (the hazy cornea of acute angle closure), which is damage, not regulation. |\n| **Protect retina from ischemia** | ⚠️ Exactly backwards, and the most clinically important error. **Raised IOP causes ischaemia**: it opposes the perfusion pressure of the ophthalmic circulation, so ocular perfusion pressure = blood pressure − IOP. That is why a pressure of 60 mmHg in acute angle closure threatens the disc and the retina, and why nocturnal hypotension with a normal IOP still damages the nerve in normal-tension glaucoma. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** IOP is what keeps the eye a rigid optical instrument, and both directions are pathological: too low and the shape fails, too high and the perfusion fails.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
    objective:'State that IOP maintains the shape and optical geometry of the globe, and correct the reversed relationships in the distractors (production, corneal thickness, perfusion).',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.143 (End Exam 3 Q30). Supporting: `L3,4) Clinical- Ocular examination` (normal range 10-21 mmHg, mean 16); ophthalmology.pdf (vitreous helps preserve the shape of the globe).'
  }